```java
class Hand implements Comparable<Hand> {
    String original;
    Card[] cards;
    int[] ranks;
    int[] suits; // not really needed except for flush check
    int[] counts; // rank -> count
    int handType;
    int[] tieBreakers;

    Hand(String original) {
        this.original = original;
        String[] parts = original.split(" ");
        cards = new Card[5];
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            cards[i] = new Card(parts[i]);
        }
        ranks = new int[5];
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) ranks[i] = cards[i].rank;
        Arrays.sort(ranks);
        // sort descending for easier comparison
        for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
            int temp = ranks[i];
            ranks[i] = ranks[4 - i];
            ranks[4 - i] = temp;
        }
        // Actually, just sort ascending and reverse, or use Integer[] and Collections.reverseOrder()
        // Let's just sort ascending and handle manually, or reverse.
        // Simpler: sort ascending, then reverse.
        Arrays.sort(ranks);
        int[] tempRanks = new int[5];
        for(int i=0; i<5; i++) tempRanks[i] = ranks[4-i];
        ranks = tempRanks;
        
        // Check flush
        boolean isFlush = true;
        char firstSuit = cards[0].suit;
        for (Card c : cards) if (c.suit != firstSuit) { isFlush = false; break; }
        
        // Check straight
        boolean isStraight = true;
        for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
            if (ranks[i] - ranks[i+1] != 1) {
                // Check wheel: A, 5, 4, 3, 2 -> ranks would be 14, 5, 4, 3, 2
                if (i == 0 && ranks[0] == 14 && ranks[1] == 5 && ranks[2] == 4 && ranks[3] == 3 && ranks[4] == 2)
{
                    isStraight = true;
                    // Adjust ranks for tie-breaking: A becomes 1, so straight high is 5
                    ranks = new int[]{5, 4, 3, 2, 1};
                } else {
                    isStraight = false;
                }
                break;
            }
        }
        
        // Count ranks
        Map<Integer, Integer> counts = new HashMap<>();
        for (int r : ranks) counts.put(r, counts.getOrDefault(r, 0) + 1);
        
        // Sort counts by value descending, then rank descending
        List<Map.Entry<Integer, Integer>> countList = new ArrayList<>(counts.entrySet());
        countList.sort((a, b) -> {
            int cmp = b.getValue().compareTo(a.getValue());
            if (cmp != 0) return cmp;
            return b.getKey().compareTo(a.getKey());
        });
        
        int[] countValues = new int[5];
        int[] countRanks = new int[5];
        for (int i = 0; i < countList.size(); i++) {
            countValues[i] = countList.get(i).getValue();
            countRanks[i] = countList.get(i).getKey();
        }
        
        // Determine hand type and tie breakers
        // We can just build an array of comparable values.
        // Let's use an int[] of length 6: [type, tb1, tb2, tb3, tb4, tb5]
        int[] score = new int[6];
        
        if (isFlush && isStraight) {
            score[0] = 9;
        } else if (countValues[0] == 4) {
            score[0] = 8;
        } else if (countValues[0] == 3 && countValues[1] == 2) {
            score[0] = 7;
        } else if (isFlush) {
            score[0] = 6;
        } else if (isStraight) {
            score[0] = 5;
        } else if (countValues[0] == 3) {
            score[0] = 4;
        } else if (countValues[0] == 2 && countValues[1] == 2) {
            score[0] = 3;
        } else if (countValues[0] == 2) {
            score[0] = 2;
        } else {
            score[0] = 1;
        }
        
        // Fill tie breakers based on type
        // For most types, tie breakers are just the ranks in order of importance.
        // But for pairs, quads, full house, 3ofk, 2pair, the order matters.
        // Actually, countRanks already gives ranks sorted by frequency desc, then rank desc.
        // This matches the tie-breaking order for all types except Flush and Straight.
        // For Flush and Straight, tie breakers are just ranks descending.
        
        if (score[0] == 6 || score[0] == 5) {
            // Flush or Straight: tie break by ranks descending
            for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) score[i+1] = ranks[i];
        } else {
            // Others: tie break by countRanks
            for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) score[i+1] = countRanks[i];
        }
        
        this.score = score;
    }
    
    int[] score;
    
    @Override
    public int compareTo(Hand other) {
        for (int i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
            if (this.score[i] != other.score[i]) {
                return Integer.compare(this.score[i], other.score[i]);
            }
        }
        return 0;
    }
}
```

Wait, `countRanks` for Two Pair: e.g., 2H 2D 4S 4C 9H -> counts: 2->2, 4->2, 9->1.
`countList` sorts by value desc, then rank desc: (2,2), (4,2), (9,1).
`countRanks` = [4, 2, 9, 0, 0].
Tie breakers for Two Pair should be: high pair, low pair, kicker. So [4, 2, 9]. This matches.
For Full House: 3->3, 2->2. `countRanks` = [3, 2, 0, 0, 0]. Matches.
For Four of a Kind: 4->4, 1->1. `countRanks` = [4, 1, 0, 0, 0]. Matches.
For Three of a Kind: 3->3, 2->1, 1->1. `countRanks` = [3, 2, 1, 0, 0]. Matches.
For One Pair: 2->2, 5->1, 4->1, 3->1. `countRanks` = [2, 5, 4, 3, 0]. Matches.
So `countRanks` works perfectly for all non-flush/non-straight hands.

One detail: `ranks` array modification for wheel straight.
If wheel straight: `ranks` becomes `[5, 4, 3, 2, 1]`.
`countRanks` will be `[5, 4, 3, 2, 1]` because all counts are 1.
So it works.

